WEBVTT

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:02.241
Imagine you run an online shop.

00:00:02.466 --> 00:00:05.927
Orders are slow to look up,
so you add a database index.

00:00:06.152 --> 00:00:08.968
Could building it stop shoppers
from placing new orders?

00:00:09.623 --> 00:00:11.395
Let's check that change with ArchGuard.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.277
We'll run a demo in the browser,

00:00:13.347 --> 00:00:16.846
fix a migration locally,
and try a capacity estimate.

00:00:17.072 --> 00:00:20.081
You can follow the browser steps
without installing anything.

00:00:21.376 --> 00:00:24.244
Open the ArchGuard explorer
from the repository.

00:00:24.469 --> 00:00:26.937
In the live playground, click List Tools.

00:00:27.162 --> 00:00:30.030
These are the checks a connected
coding assistant can call.

00:00:30.769 --> 00:00:32.580
Click Demo: Audit SQL.

00:00:32.805 --> 00:00:36.652
The example builds an index on
orders and drops an old column.

00:00:36.878 --> 00:00:40.054
ArchGuard reads those
statements and returns a report.

00:00:40.756 --> 00:00:43.005
Start with the verdict: block deploy.

00:00:43.231 --> 00:00:45.583
The index build can hold up new writes.

00:00:45.834 --> 00:00:49.625
The column change is a separate
risk: an older copy of the app might

00:00:49.635 --> 00:00:50.853
still try to read it.

00:00:52.176 --> 00:00:53.665
Let's isolate the index.

00:00:53.903 --> 00:00:58.343
With Node twenty or newer, open a
terminal in the cloned repository.

00:00:58.581 --> 00:01:01.681
Save this one statement in orders dot SQL,

00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:04.103
then run the migrate command shown here.

00:01:04.823 --> 00:01:06.518
The command reads the file.

00:01:06.743 --> 00:01:10.655
It returns exit code one and
flags the blocking index build.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.915
It also points out that the
script has no lock timeout.

00:01:14.623 --> 00:01:17.697
Add a short lock timeout to
bound the wait for a lock.

00:01:17.922 --> 00:01:21.189
Change the index build to
create index concurrently,

00:01:21.298 --> 00:01:24.063
and run it outside an explicit transaction.

00:01:24.756 --> 00:01:27.237
Save the file and run the same check again.

00:01:27.475 --> 00:01:31.748
This time ArchGuard reports zero
findings and exits successfully.

00:01:31.986 --> 00:01:33.900
That's a pass on its static checks.

00:01:34.138 --> 00:01:37.895
Still rehearse the migration
against a representative database.

00:01:39.189 --> 00:01:42.340
Back in the explorer,
choose Demo: Uber Math.

00:01:42.566 --> 00:01:46.142
This is a ride-dispatch scenario
with preset assumptions.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.157
Start with the assumptions
before reading the answer.

00:01:49.836 --> 00:01:54.207
The preset uses forty million daily
users and one hundred eighty writes

00:01:54.217 --> 00:01:55.332
per user per day.

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:58.116
That's seven point two
billion writes per day,

00:01:58.328 --> 00:02:01.428
or about eighty-three thousand
each second on average.

00:02:02.169 --> 00:02:05.269
The preset assumes a peak
four times the average.

00:02:05.494 --> 00:02:08.989
You get roughly three hundred
thirty-three thousand writes per second at

00:02:08.999 --> 00:02:09.599
peak.

00:02:09.824 --> 00:02:13.271
That multiplier is an input you
can change for your own service.

00:02:14.576 --> 00:02:17.676
A local audit can also find
code that's tied in a knot.

00:02:17.927 --> 00:02:20.717
Here, orders imports the price calculator,

00:02:20.814 --> 00:02:23.720
and pricing imports a
currency constant from orders.

00:02:24.035 --> 00:02:26.542
Follow the arrows and you
end up where you started.

00:02:27.223 --> 00:02:30.950
Run the audit on this example
directory and the report counts one

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.372
circular dependency.

00:02:32.597 --> 00:02:35.632
Move the shared constant
into a small money module,

00:02:35.806 --> 00:02:38.106
update the import, and run it again.

00:02:38.344 --> 00:02:40.245
The cycle count falls to zero.

00:02:41.576 --> 00:02:43.632
To use the same checks in an editor,

00:02:43.832 --> 00:02:49.109
add the remote endpoint ending in
slash M C P to its MCP server settings.

00:02:49.334 --> 00:02:53.181
MCP is the protocol that lets
your assistant call these tools.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:57.681
Try asking your assistant to check
a proposed Postgres migration and

00:02:57.691 --> 00:02:59.244
explain the first finding.

00:02:59.508 --> 00:03:03.329
For a full repository audit, use
the local command shown here.

00:03:03.555 --> 00:03:07.930
A good first exercise is to fix one
finding and run the check again.
